overview:
Correction to the previous sweep: Porphyry's Homeric Questions on the Odyssey already exist in Russian. O. P. Tsybenko translated them in Порфирий. Труды, vol. II (Квадривиум, 2019). The same volume contains the Iliad questions and De antro. Small Homer-related fragment collections are also in Russian in ΣΧΟΛΗ (Heraclides Ponticus, Ptolemy Chennus, Proclus' Vita Homeri).

Apart from these, I found no Russian translation of the following:
- any book of the Odyssey scholia;
- Eustathius on the Odyssey;
- the Aristarchean fragment collections (Aristonicus, Didymus, Nicanor);
- Apollonius Sophista;
- the Homeric fragments of Crates of Mallos;
- the papyrus hypomnemata.

English:
- Eustathius on the Odyssey: books 1–12 (Brill vols I–III, 2022–2026; the series runs to about 2030).
- Odyssey D-scholia: only the mythographic narratives (Roy & Smith 2026, CC BY).
- Aristotle's and Heraclides' Homeric fragments: translated.
- Porphyry: only the Iliad (MacPhail 2011). The Odyssey part has no English.

No modern-language translation of the major Odyssey scholia was found. Pontani's edition (vols I–VI, books 1–12, 2007–2025) has no translation. Two Barcelona (UAB) doctoral theses give open critical texts of the scholia: Od. 9 (CC BY-NC-ND) and Od. 10 (CC BY-SA). For books 13–24 the only full edition is Dindorf 1855, which is public domain. The best gaps are book-units of the scholia, Aristonicus, Crates, and Eustathius on Od. 13–24.
